{"simulation_proposals":[{"request_identifier":"lcg_die_fairness_10000","hypothesis":"The specified linear congruential generator produces a fair distribution over six faces after 10000 draws, where fair means every face count falls within two standard deviations of the uniform expectation (expectation 10000/6 ≈ 1666.67, standard deviation sqrt(10000 * 1/6 * 5/6) ≈ 37.27). The exact counts cannot be derived in closed form but must be computed by running the exact recurrence.","rival_predictions":["The generator is unfair because LCG output modulo 6 does not uniformly cover all residues when the modulus 2^31 is not divisible by 6, introducing a systematic bias that may push at least one face count outside the two-sigma tolerance.","The generator is fair because the bias from the non-divisible modulus is negligibly small at n=10000 relative to the two-sigma tolerance of ~74.5.","The generator is unfair due to the short period and low-order bit correlations of the LCG, regardless of the modulus-divisibility argument."],"discriminating_purpose":"Produce the exact six face counts and a calibration trace of the first ten (X_n, die_n) pairs so that the recurrence implementation and face-mapping can be verified by hand, and the fairness verdict can be adjudicated by applying the two-standard-deviation tolerance to the actual counts.","simulation_mode":"sandboxed_python_v1","model_source":"def simulate(inputs, rng):\n    x = 42\n    counts = [0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0]\n    cal = {}\n    for n in range(1, 11):\n        x = (1103515245 * x + 12345) % (2 ** 31)\n        die = 1 + (x % 6)\n        cal['x_%d' % n] = x\n        cal['die_%d' % n] = die\n    for n in range(11, 10001):\n        x = (1103515245 * x + 12345) % (2 ** 31)\n        die = 1 + (x % 6)\n        counts[die - 1] += 1\n    for n in range(1, 11):\n        x_val = cal['x_%d' % n]\n        die_val = cal['die_%d' % n]\n        counts[die_val - 1] += 1\n    result = {}\n    for i in range(6):\n        result['count_%d' % (i + 1)] = counts[i]\n    result['total'] = sum(counts)\n    for n in range(1, 11):\n        result['cal_x_%d' % n] = cal['x_%d' % n]\n        result['cal_die_%d' % n] = cal['die_%d' % n]\n    return result","parameter_definitions":[{"name":"default","values_json":"{}"}],"requested_observables":["count_1","count_2","count_3","count_4","count_5","count_6","total","cal_x_1","cal_die_1","cal_x_2","cal_die_2","cal_x_3","cal_die_3","cal_x_4","cal_die_4","cal_x_5","cal_die_5","cal_x_6","cal_die_6","cal_x_7","cal_die_7","cal_x_8","cal_die_8","cal_x_9","cal_die_9","cal_x_10","cal_die_10"],"requested_seed_set":[0],"declared_assumptions":["Integer arithmetic is exact and unbounded in the sandbox (Python int).","The recurrence X_(n+1) = (1103515245 * X_n + 12345) mod 2^31 with X_0 = 42 is the sole source of pseudorandomness.","The face mapping die_n = 1 + (X_n mod 6) is applied to every X_n for n = 1..10000.","The fairness criterion is applied post-hoc by the harness: every count_1..count_6 must fall within [10000/6 - 2*sqrt(10000*1/6*5/6), 10000/6 + 2*sqrt(10000*1/6*5/6)]."],"interpretation_conditions":["The calibration trace (cal_x_1..cal_x_10, cal_die_1..cal_die_10) must match a hand-computed application of the recurrence from X_0 = 42.","The total observable must equal 10000 exactly.","The six count_ observables must sum to 10000.","The fairness verdict is adjudicated by the harness applying the two-standard-deviation tolerance to the returned counts; the simulation itself does not return a boolean."]}],"candidates":[{"claim":"The LCG will be fair because the modulus-divisibility bias is negligible at n=10000 relative to a two-sigma tolerance of ~74.5.","mechanism":"The modulus 2^31 = 2147483648; dividing by 6 gives 357913941 remainder 2. Residues 0 and 1 each get one extra integer in the partition, so their corresponding faces are over-represented by a fraction of 2/(6*2^31) ≈ 1.55e-10. Over 10000 draws the expected excess is ~1.55e-6 counts — utterly negligible compared to the tolerance band.","counterconditions":["If the actual counts show any face outside [1592.1, 1741.2], the fairness claim is refuted.","If the LCG has structural correlations (e.g., short effective period modulo 6) that amplify the bias beyond the negligible per-draw amount, the fairness claim is refuted."],"typicality":0.8,"evidence_refs":[{"block":"c6c31b919b1cd091","quote":"This problem cannot be answered without it: the exact face-count distribution of a specific pseudorandom sequence has no closed form to evaluate by hand at n=10000 terms."},{"block":"ff5f75b13b928302","quote":"Implement the recurrence exactly as specified, run it for the full n, and RETURN the six face counts as flat observables (`count_1` .. `count_6`) plus `total`"}],"optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive","requested_context_aliases":[]}},{"claim":"The LCG will be unfair because the non-divisible modulus introduces a systematic bias that, combined with LCG structural correlations, pushes at least one face count outside two standard deviations.","mechanism":"Because 2^31 mod 6 = 2, the partition of the 2^31 integers into 6 bins is uneven: two faces get ceil(2^31/6) integers and four get floor(2^31/6). This creates a per-draw bias. Moreover, LCGs are known to have poor low-bit periodicity; the face mapping X mod 6 depends on low bits, so the bias may be amplified over 10000 draws beyond the naive per-draw estimate.","counterconditions":["If the actual counts all fall within [1592.1, 1741.2], the unfairness claim is refuted.","If the LCG's structural correlations are weaker than expected and the modulus bias remains negligible at n=10000, the unfairness claim is refuted."],"typicality":0.65,"evidence_refs":[{"block":"c6c31b919b1cd091","quote":"This problem cannot be answered without it: the exact face-count distribution of a specific pseudorandom sequence has no closed form to evaluate by hand at n=10000 terms."}],"optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive","requested_context_aliases":[]}},{"claim":"The LCG will be fair simply because it is a well-known generator (the glibc LCG) designed to pass basic statistical tests, and 10000 draws is well within its period of 2^31.","mechanism":"The multiplier 1103515245 and increment 12345 are the parameters of the glibc rand() implementation, which has been used for decades in statistical applications. For a sample size of 10000 — tiny relative to the period of 2^31 — the output should be statistically indistinguishable from uniform over six faces.","counterconditions":["If the actual counts show any face outside [1592.1, 1741.2], the fairness claim is refuted.","If empirical testing reveals that the specific seed 42 produces an anomalous sequence despite the generator's general reputation, the fairness claim is refuted.","If the mod-6 reduction extracts low-order bits known to be weak in this LCG, the fairness claim is refuted."],"typicality":0.5,"evidence_refs":[{"block":"c6c31b919b1cd091","quote":"This problem cannot be answered without it: the exact face-count distribution of a specific pseudorandom sequence has no closed form to evaluate by hand at n=10000 terms."}],"optional_refs":["SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive","requested_context_aliases":[]}},{"claim":"The fairness question is a category error: a deterministic PRNG cannot be 'fair' in the probabilistic sense; it produces one fixed sequence. The two-sigma criterion is a frequency test applied to one realization, and the verdict depends entirely on the actual computed counts, not on any probabilistic property of the generator.","mechanism":"The LCG is deterministic. For seed 42, the 10000-draw sequence is fixed. The 'fairness' criterion is a post-hoc frequency check, not a property of the generator. The only way to adjudicate is to run the exact recurrence and apply the tolerance to the resulting counts.","counterconditions":["If one interprets 'fair' as a property of the generator's output distribution over all seeds (a frequentist ensemble), then the question becomes whether the generator's design ensures most seeds produce fair sequences, which requires a different analysis.","If the actual counts happen to fall within tolerance, the verdict is 'fair' for this realization; if not, 'unfair' — but this is an observation, not a prediction."],"typicality":0.3,"evidence_refs":[{"block":"a3db120ba929af11","quote":"Return the discriminating quantity itself -- the six counts, the calibration trace -- never a boolean summarizing them."},{"block":"c6c31b919b1cd091","quote":"This problem cannot be answered without it: the exact face-count distribution of a specific pseudorandom sequence has no closed form to evaluate by hand at n=10000 terms."}],"optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive","requested_context_aliases":[]}},{"claim":"The seed X_0 = 42 itself may produce an anomalous early sequence that skews the face distribution, regardless of the generator's general statistical properties.","mechanism":"Specific seeds in LCGs can produce sequences with atypical early-term correlations. Since the face mapping depends on X mod 6, and the low bits of LCGs are known to have short periods, seed 42 may produce a subsequence where certain residues are over- or under-represented in the first 10000 terms. The only way to test this is to run the exact recurrence from this seed.","counterconditions":["If the actual counts all fall within [1592.1, 1741.2], the seed-anomaly claim is refuted.","If the seed 42 produces a sequence statistically typical of the generator's output over 10000 draws, the claim is refuted."],"typicality":0.2,"evidence_refs":[{"block":"c6c31b919b1cd091","quote":"This problem cannot be answered without it: the exact face-count distribution of a specific pseudorandom sequence has no closed form to evaluate by hand at n=10000 terms."}],"optional_refs":["SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive","requested_context_aliases":[]}}]}